### What are terminal operations?

Terminal operations trigger the entire lazy pipeline, produce a result, and consume the stream. After a terminal, the stream cannot be reused.

### How does collect work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CollectDemo {
    record Transaction(String symbol, BigDecimal amount, boolean settled) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(
            new Transaction("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("45000"), true),
            new Transaction("TCS", new BigDecimal("78000"), true),
            new Transaction("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("32000"), false),
            new Transaction("HDFC", new BigDecimal("120000"), true)
        );

        // To List:
        List<String> symbols = txns.stream().map(Transaction::symbol).toList();

        // To Set:
        Set<String> unique = txns.stream().map(Transaction::symbol).collect(Collectors.toSet());

        // Grouping:
        Map<String, List<Transaction>> bySymbol = txns.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Transaction::symbol));

        // Joining:
        String csv = txns.stream().map(Transaction::symbol)
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

        System.out.println("Symbols: " + symbols);
        System.out.println("Unique: " + unique);
        System.out.println("CSV: " + csv);
    }
}
```

```text
Symbols: [RELIANCE, TCS, RELIANCE, HDFC]
Unique: [RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC]
CSV: RELIANCE, TCS, RELIANCE, HDFC
```

### How does reduce work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ReduceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> amounts = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("45000"), new BigDecimal("78000"), new BigDecimal("120000")
        );

        // With identity: always returns a value
        BigDecimal sum = amounts.stream()
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

        // Without identity: returns Optional (handles empty stream)
        Optional<BigDecimal> max = amounts.stream()
            .reduce(BigDecimal::max);

        System.out.println("Sum: Rs." + sum);
        System.out.println("Max: Rs." + max.orElse(BigDecimal.ZERO));
    }
}
```

```text
Sum: Rs.243000
Max: Rs.120000
```

### How do short-circuit terminals work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class ShortCircuitTerminals {
    record Transaction(String id, BigDecimal amount, boolean settled) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(
            new Transaction("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"), false),
            new Transaction("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("45000"), true),
            new Transaction("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("78000"), true)
        );

        // anyMatch: stops on first true
        boolean hasSettled = txns.stream().anyMatch(Transaction::settled);

        // allMatch: stops on first false
        boolean allSettled = txns.stream().allMatch(Transaction::settled);

        // noneMatch: stops on first true
        boolean noneFailed = txns.stream()
            .noneMatch(t -> t.amount().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000000")) > 0);

        System.out.println("Has settled: " + hasSettled);
        System.out.println("All settled: " + allSettled);
        System.out.println("None over 10L: " + noneFailed);
    }
}
```

```text
Has settled: true
All settled: false
None over 10L: true
```

### What is the toMap duplicate key pitfall?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ToMapPitfall {
    record Transaction(String symbol, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(
            new Transaction("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("45000")),
            new Transaction("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("32000")) // duplicate key!
        );

        // WRONG: throws IllegalStateException on duplicate key
        // Map<String, BigDecimal> bad = txns.stream()
        //     .collect(Collectors.toMap(Transaction::symbol, Transaction::amount));

        // CORRECT: merge function handles duplicates
        Map<String, BigDecimal> good = txns.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Transaction::symbol,
                Transaction::amount,
                BigDecimal::add)); // merge: sum amounts for same symbol

        System.out.println(good); // {RELIANCE=77000}
    }
}
```

```text
{RELIANCE=77000}
```

### What is the difference between forEach and forEachOrdered?

In sequential streams both preserve order. In parallel streams, forEach is non-deterministic (faster), forEachOrdered preserves encounter order (slower). Prefer collect(toList()) over forEachOrdered for ordered results from parallel streams.

### What should a senior engineer know?

collect is the most powerful terminal because Collectors provide groupingBy, partitioningBy, toMap, joining, summarising, and downstream composition. reduce is for simple aggregation (sum, max, concatenation). count is straightforward but triggers the full pipeline. For the common pattern "check if at least one matches," prefer anyMatch over findAny().isPresent() for clarity.
